<section class="eg-section" id="pain">
  <div class="eg-container">
    <div class="eg-section-head eg-section-head-wide">
      <div><span class="eg-eyebrow">Your workflow. A shared foundation.</span><h2>Spend your design effort on the application.</h2></div>
      <p>LangGraph gives you graph execution. EZGraph supplies conventions for the application around it—so the next stage, and the next workflow, follow a structure your team already knows.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="eg-three">
      <div class="eg-pillar"><span class="eg-pillar-number">01 / BUILD CONSISTENTLY</span><h3>One contract across stages.</h3><p>Keep a stage's prompt, tools, validation, state writes, and lifecycle hooks in an <code>LlmNode</code>. Read one node and know where to find the same responsibilities in another.</p><a class="eg-link" href="/ezgraph/why/#node-contract">Explore the contract <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
      <div class="eg-pillar"><span class="eg-pillar-number">02 / CONTROL EACH STAGE</span><h3>Give the model a focused job.</h3><p>Select the tools and history that belong to the current stage. Let models handle recognition and ambiguity; let your code validate facts, enforce policy, and decide what happens next.</p><a class="eg-link" href="/ezgraph/why/#scoped-tools">Explore stage control <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
      <div class="eg-pillar"><span class="eg-pillar-number">03 / INSPECT THE APPLICATION</span><h3>Understand a saved session.</h3><p>A readable document brings together the active node, business facts, histories, usage, and diagnostics. Give a developer—or an AI debugging assistant—the application record to inspect.</p><a class="eg-link" href="/ezgraph/why/#session-document">Explore session inspection <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
